{
  "abstract": "We rebuild curvature coupling and structural transitions on the corrected FP\u2013I\u2013III spine. A Laplace-type operator is normalized as L=\\nabla^\\ast\\nabla+\\mathcal R. Curvature can shift the spectral cluster, mix coherent and noncoherent sectors, and create a leakage floor. If \\|\\mathcal R\\|<\\lambda_\\ast/2, the full curved operator retains a separated low cluster and defines a new Riesz projector; if one retains the unperturbed projector, the term Q\\mathcal RP must appear explicitly. Centroids are defined intrinsically by a Karcher mean inside a convex normal ball. A first-order gradient parent flow yields a first-order modulation law; a Newtonian law requires a separately specified inertial parent equation. Absolute interaction bounds give |E_{\\rm int}|\\le C\\mathcal O, while attraction or repulsion needs a sign hypothesis. Structural transitions are controlled by a Lyapunov mountain- pass/work theorem, and exit detection is separated from selection of the post-transition basin.",
